---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Photo from "../components/Photo.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";

// Charity not chosen yet. Set these once the family decides.
const CHARITY = { name: "", url: "" };
---
<Base title="Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, 1929 to 2026">
  <Nav current="obituary" />

  <header class="cover">
    <div class="cover-media" data-cover-media>
      <div class="photo-plate"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="cover-title col">
      <span class="lady" data-reveal>Lady</span>
      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Some people pass through life quietly. Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns was not one of them.</p>
      <p>Margaret passed away peacefully in Montreal on November [X], 2026, surrounded by family, at the age of 97. Hers was a remarkable life that spanned nearly a century, crossed an ocean, and held more reinventions, heartbreaks, and triumphs than most people could imagine.</p>
    </div>

    <Photo size="full" ratio="16 / 9" />

    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="dropcap">Born in Glasgow, Scotland, in 1929, Margaret was one of seven children raised amid the poverty and religious divisions of the city’s working-class communities. Her childhood offered little in the way of comfort or certainty. Her father, Hugh Cairns, was in and out of prison and struggled with alcoholism, while her mother, Helen Kennedy, died suddenly when Margaret was just six years old. The Kennedy-Cairns children were left to navigate a precarious existence, relying on one another in circumstances that demanded resourcefulness well beyond their years.</p>
      <p>The Second World War brought further hardship. As German bombs fell on Glasgow, Margaret came of age in circumstances of unimaginable adversity. Yet even amid those impossible beginnings, the qualities that would define her life were taking shape: an indomitable will, a fierce independence, and a determination to make something more of the hand she’d been dealt.</p>
      <p>Margaret came from a long line of strong, remarkable women. Her maternal grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, was a formidable figure in Scotland’s women’s Orange Order, rising to become a senior leader of the organization, a woman of considerable standing in a world far removed from the poverty in which Margaret and her siblings were raised. Margaret recalled that her father largely kept her grandmother away from the children, but she grew up aware of her importance. It was an early glimpse of the influence and independence that women of her generation were so rarely afforded, and of the religious tensions that shaped Margaret’s world: her grandmother’s Protestant Orange Order identity stood in marked contrast to the Catholic tradition in which Margaret was raised, a divide that ran through Scottish family and community life as both a source of belonging and a boundary between people.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- The crossing: one photograph holds the screen while the passage scrolls over it. -->
    <section class="sticky-scene">
      <div class="sticky-media"><div class="photo-plate"></div></div>
      <div class="sticky-steps">
        <p class="step">Raised Catholic, Margaret was studying to become a nun before, at nineteen, she made a rather different decision: to cross the Atlantic alone and begin a new life in Montreal, where she would marry a man she barely knew.</p>
        <p class="step">An ocean now separated her from her brothers and sisters, the people with whom she had survived the hardships of childhood.</p>
        <p class="step">In the years that followed, she would raise three children within that first marriage, far from the familiar support of her family. Her husband suffered from a brain tumour, and family accounts describe him as abusive.</p>
        <p class="step">These were lonely and difficult beginnings for a young woman building a home in a new country. Making Montreal her own would take more than the courage to board a ship. It would take years of finding her footing and building a life she could call her own.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Montreal: photographs change in place as each passage arrives. -->
    <section class="sequence">
      <div class="sequence-media">
        <div class="seq-frame is-active" data-seq="0"><div class="photo-plate"></div></div>
        <div class="seq-frame" data-seq="1"><div class="photo-plate"></div></div>
        <div class="seq-frame" data-seq="2"><div class="photo-plate"></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sequence-steps prose">
        <p data-step="0">A model in the 1950s, Margaret had striking Scottish looks: vivid red hair, expressive features, and a smile that could light up a room. She carried that beauty and a youthful radiance well into her later years, along with an instinctive appreciation for elegance and presentation that never left her.</p>
        <p data-step="1">She was glamorous, opinionated, endlessly sociable, and blessed with a personality that could fill a room before she’d properly entered it. She loved beautiful things, good conversation, and the pleasure of making an impression. Admirers were rarely in short supply; Margaret married four times, and there were plenty of men happy to chase her along the way. She had an eye for style, a gift for storytelling, and an almost supernatural ability to turn an ordinary encounter into an occasion.</p>
        <p data-step="2">She was quick to laugh, and occasionally quicker to anger, and possessed of a Scottish wit as sharp as her spectacularly stubborn opinions. Her presence was unmistakable, her appetite for life difficult to contain, and life in Margaret’s company was never dull. Whether you knew her for years or met her only briefly, Margaret was someone you remembered.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="col prose">
      <p>Music was another of Margaret’s great gifts. Blessed with a beautiful singing voice, she carried on a musical tradition that ran through her family, which also included her niece, the celebrated Scottish singer Lulu. Margaret was rarely far from a song, and her voice filled the family home with music, warmth, and life. For those who knew her, the sound of Margaret singing would become one of the most enduring memories of her presence.</p>
    </div>

    <Photo size="inset" ratio="4 / 5" />

    <div class="col prose">
      <p>Behind the glamour was a woman of considerable resourcefulness and entrepreneurial spirit. After her modelling years, Margaret worked tirelessly to provide for her four children, finding ways to make a living and create opportunities in a world that offered women far fewer paths to financial independence than it does today. She was enterprising by instinct, with a talent for connecting with people, a keen eye for opportunity, and a determination that rarely admitted defeat.</p>
      <p>Margaret belonged to a generation of women whose ambitions often exceeded the opportunities available to them. She possessed many of the qualities that might have made her a formidable entrepreneur in another era: charisma, resourcefulness, conviction, and an instinct for making things happen. Whatever the limitations of her time, she refused to let them entirely dictate the boundaries of her life.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Colin and Helen: no photograph, more space, slower. -->
    <div class="col prose hush">
      <p>Beneath that formidable exterior was a woman whose life had demanded extraordinary resilience, and whose greatest devotion was always to her family.</p>
      <p>She endured losses that no parent should ever have to bear, including the deaths of her son Colin and daughter Helen, both in their twenties, within eighteen months of one another.</p>
      <p>No amount of strength or determination could spare her that grief. Yet she carried it alongside the hardships of her early life, somehow retaining her appetite for beauty, laughter, conversation, and the company of others.</p>
    </div>

    <Photo size="wide" ratio="3 / 2" />

    <div class="col prose">
      <p>For all her independence, Margaret never hesitated to put the needs of those she loved before her own. Her generosity was not merely sentimental; it was practical, instinctive, and often deeply self-sacrificing. When her family needed her, she showed up. At one point, she left her beloved Montreal to live in New York City, helping to raise her grandson, Sean.</p>
      <p>It was a chapter that spoke to the depth of her devotion: a woman who had spent her life fiercely guarding her independence, willingly setting it aside for the people who mattered most. Her love was expressed not only in words or affection, but in the countless ways she made herself indispensable to those around her.</p>
      <p>Faith remained a central thread throughout Margaret’s life, though her spiritual journey was as independent-minded as the woman herself. Having grown up in the Catholic tradition, she discovered Christian Science in the 1970s and became a deeply devoted student of the teachings of Mary Baker Eddy. Her faith was neither casual nor merely ceremonial; she approached it with intellectual curiosity, conviction, and a lifelong desire to understand the relationship between mind, spirit, and the human experience.</p>
      <p>In later life, Margaret’s family gifted her a small plot of land in her native Scotland, along with a certificate bestowing upon her the honorary title of Lady Margaret. It was a playful distinction, but one that suited her splendidly. The girl who had left Glasgow with little to her name had, in her own way, become a lady. Though those who knew her might reasonably argue that she had always carried herself like one.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pair-row">
      <Photo size="inset" ratio="4 / 5" />
      <Photo size="inset" ratio="4 / 5" />
    </div>

    <div class="col prose">
      <p>Above all, Margaret loved her family. Her affection was fierce, her loyalty unwavering, and her generosity boundless. Those closest to her knew a woman of wonderful contradictions: tender and tempestuous, elegant and irreverent, independent and deeply attached. She could be demanding, exasperating, and gloriously entertaining, sometimes all in the same afternoon. But no one who belonged to Margaret’s family ever had reason to doubt the depth of her love.</p>
      <p>In her later years, as age gradually diminished the independence she had guarded so fiercely, the people who loved her remained close. But those final years were only a small part of a much larger story.</p>
      <p>Her life carried her from the poverty of wartime Glasgow to the elegance of mid-century Montreal, through extraordinary social transformations, personal reinventions, heartbreaks, and triumphs. She had been a model, a mother, a grandmother, a woman of faith, and a force of nature.</p>
    </div>

    <Photo size="full" ratio="16 / 9" />

    <div class="col prose">
      <p>She was predeceased by her beloved children, Colin and Helen; her longtime partner, George Kendall; and her siblings, including her sister Nellie and brother James. She is survived by her son, Paul, her daughter, Anthea, and her grandson, Sean, who will carry forward her memory with enormous love, admiration, laughter, and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated, and her ashes scattered on Mount Royal, where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>Even in her final days, her wit was as sharp as ever. When nurses explained that they needed to take medical images of her ribs, the former model, without missing a beat, asked:</p>
    </div>

    <section class="closing">
      <blockquote data-split>“Am I being paid for these pictures?”</blockquote>
      <p><i>Some things, it seems, never change.</i></p>
    </section>

    <div class="col prose to-book">
      <p><a href="/memories">Share a memory of Margaret, or read what others have written, in the memory book.</a></p>
    </div>

    {CHARITY.name && (
      <section class="col prose tribute">
        <p>In lieu of flowers, the family asks that you consider a donation to {CHARITY.name}, a cause close to Margaret’s heart.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={CHARITY.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donate to {CHARITY.name}</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </main>

  <footer class="col">
    <p>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · Glasgow 1929 · Montreal 2026</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/motion.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
